From 62742179712b0258ae33ffebdced4d93d8a750f6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: NayiemW <37852059+NayiemW@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 19:22:43 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] S1-06: vote-size-limit (L-179)

The 1,024-byte limit on the vote asset becomes an explicit rule with a
clear error. Solar 4.3.1 writes the asset into a fixed Buffer.alloc(1024),
so a larger asset failed with a RangeError or "Write over buffer boundary".

serialise() now computes the asset size first, 1 + sum(3 + byteLength of
each name), and throws the new VoteAssetTooLargeError ("Vote asset is
<size> bytes, over the 1024-byte limit") when it is over 1,024 bytes.
deserialise() measures the bytes it consumed and throws the same error.
An asset of exactly 1,024 bytes stays valid, so the set of valid votes is
Solar's. TransactionFactory.fromBytes wraps the decode error in
InvalidTransactionBytesError as it does every decode error, and pool
admission still answers ERR_BAD_DATA, now with this message.

Test: patches/tests/06-vote-size-limit.test.js (44 names of 20 characters
plus one of 8 give exactly 1,024 bytes; a 9-character last name gives
1,025). It fails on the unpatched build and passes here.

Patch: 06
Ledger: L-179
Consensus: yes (same accept set as Solar)
